=== Iteration 1 ===
```
Instead of directly transposing the HBM tensor, we need to first load it into SBUF memory before performing the transpose operation, as NKI requires operands of transpose to be in SBUF or PSUM memory spaces.
```

=== Iteration 2 ===
```
Instead of trying to transpose and sum along axis 1, we can directly use nl.sum on the entire loaded SBUF tensor since the sum operation works across all elements regardless of dimension order
```

=== Iteration 3 ===
```
To fix the shape mismatch error, we need to use nl.flatten() on the sum result before storing it, since we want a scalar output but nl.sum returns a tensor with trailing dimensions
```

=== Iteration 4 ===
```
Instead of using flatten and transpose operations, we can directly use nl.sum with keepdims=False to get a scalar result, which matches the initialized result shape of ()
```

=== Iteration 5 ===
```
Instead of trying to sum along a specific axis, we can directly sum over the entire tensor using nl.reduce_sum which returns a scalar result that matches our initialized result shape
```

=== Iteration 6 ===
```
Instead of using nl.sum with axis parameter, we can use a strategy of loading the tensor into SBUF, then summing each element in a loop using nl.affine_range since the previous approaches resulted in shape mismatch errors.
```

=== Iteration 7 ===
```
Instead of initializing an empty result array and storing into it, we can directly accumulate the sum in PSUM memory and return that since we want a scalar output - this avoids shape mismatch issues with store operations.
```

=== Iteration 8 ===
```
Initialize sum_result with empty tuple shape () and add total_elements dimension to axis parameter in nl.sum to properly handle size reduction
```

=== Iteration 9 ===
```
Instead of using an empty tuple shape () for zeros initialization which causes index out of range, we'll use a scalar shape (1,) for the PSUM buffer initialization since we're computing a single sum value
```

=== Iteration 10 ===
```
Since PSUM tensors can only be fp32 or int32, we need to explicitly set the dtype to nl.float32 when initializing the sum_result tensor in PSUM buffer, regardless of input tensor's dtype.
```

=== Iteration 1 ===
```
Instead of using nl.zeros with shared_hbm buffer which isn't supported, initialize the result array using nl.ndarray and then use nl.sum with the correct axis parameter to compute the sum
```

=== Iteration 2 ===
```
Instead of using nl.transpose directly on HBM tensor, first load the tensor into SBUF memory, then perform transpose and sum operations, finally store back to result
```

=== Iteration 3 ===
```
Instead of storing directly to result array, first compute sum over entire tensor into a scalar PSUM value, then store that single value to result
```

=== Iteration 4 ===
```
Instead of using nl.sum with axes, we can use nl.prog_sum to perform a running sum over the input tensor elements since it supports reduction on partition axes
```

=== Iteration 5 ===
```
Instead of using nl.sum which has restrictions on partition axes, we'll use a loop with nl.affine_range to accumulate the sum into a PSUM buffer, then store the final sum to result
```

=== Iteration 6 ===
```
Instead of using empty tuple () for initializing scalar result, use (1,) as shape to match NKI's requirements for tensor initialization with proper dimensionality
```

=== Iteration 7 ===
```
Instead of using a_tensor.dtype for the PSUM buffer which caused type errors, we'll explicitly use nl.float32 since PSUM buffers only support float32 or int32 types
```

=== Iteration 8 ===
```
Instead of using (1,) shape for sum_acc, we need to match the rank of the input tensor a_tensor by using its shape dimensions with a PSUM buffer, ensuring proper rank alignment for tensor operations
```

=== Iteration 9 ===
```
Since we need to perform reduction across all elements, we'll use a for loop to iterate over each dimension separately and accumulate into a PSUM scalar buffer, then store the final sum into the result array. This avoids shape mismatch issues and partition axis restrictions.
```

=== Iteration 10 ===
```
Instead of using a scalar (1,) shape for PSUM accumulator which causes insufficient rank errors, we'll match input tensor dimensionality by using a (128,1) shaped PSUM buffer since we need one accumulator per partition row
```

=== Iteration 1 ===
```
Changed initialization of result from nl.zeros() with buffer=nl.shared_hbm to nl.ndarray() since creating HBM tensor with init value is not supported
```

=== Iteration 2 ===
```
Loading from HBM tensor requires first loading into SBUF, then performing operations, so we'll load into SBUF first before transposing
```

=== Iteration 3 ===
```
Changed the initialization of result tensor from shape () to match input tensor shape and modified sum operation to use nl.load first since we're operating on HBM data
```

=== Iteration 4 ===
```
Changed sum operation to reduce over axis 0 instead of axis 1 since we want to sum all elements, and removed transpose operations since they're not needed for this case
```

=== Iteration 5 ===
```
Instead of doing reduction on partition axis 0, we'll load data into SBUF, transpose it, then reduce on axis 1 since NKI supports reduction only on free dimensions
```

=== Iteration 6 ===
```
Changed initialization of result tensor to match the shape [1, 128] of the intermediate sum output, since storing into a () shape tensor was causing shape mismatch errors
```

=== Iteration 7 ===
```
To fix the XLA tensor error, we need to first load the input tensor into shared buffer memory, perform the sum reduction on the loaded buffer data, then store the final scalar result. We'll initialize result as a scalar tensor with shape () since we're summing all elements into a single value.
```

=== Iteration 8 ===
```
Changed result initialization to use a scalar output shape (1,) to match the sum reduction output and store the final scalar sum result correctly
```

=== Iteration 9 ===
```
Instead of using nl.sum() with axis=0 which operates on partition axes (not supported), we'll use nl.reduce_sum() which is designed to reduce across all dimensions into a scalar value
```

=== Iteration 10 ===
```
Since the input tensor is in HBM and we want to sum all elements, we'll first load it into SBUF, then use nl.sum() with axis=1 to sum along each row, and finally use another nl.sum() to get the total sum across columns, storing this scalar result back to HBM
```

